<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sviatopolk II of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1050–<a href="/years/1113">1113</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sviatopolk Iziaslavich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sviatopolk II Iziaslavich was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from 1093 until his death. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-liubech">Lyubech Congress</a> of 1097, where the Rus' princes agreed to hold their fathers' lands, and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> in campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>. His death was followed by an uprising in Kiev and the accession of Monomakh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sviatopolk-ii-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Sviatopolk II of Kiev" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Лобачев Владимир · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Sviatopolk_II_of_Kiev_(drawing).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sviatopolk was the son of Iziaslav Iaroslavich by a concubine. While his brother Iaropolk lived, he was not regarded as a possible claimant to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. In 1069 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, a city his father had briefly taken from its ruler Vseslav, and for ten years, 1078–88, he ruled Novgorod. When his brother died he succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a>, which stayed in the possession of his descendants until the 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Vsevolod Iaroslavich died in 1093, the other princes acknowledged Sviatopolk as the senior son of a grand prince and let him take the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. He was not a popular prince, and his reign was marked by constant rivalry with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>, although he took part in the princely congresses that Monomakh organised. He is sometimes charged with encouraging wars among the Rurikid princes: with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> he took part in capturing and blinding a Galician prince.</p><p>Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> he was less fortunate. He was defeated in 1093 at the Battle of the Stugna River and again later that year, after which the Kipchaks destroyed Torchesk, an Oghuz Turk settlement. In 1096, while trying to force Oleg I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> into an agreement among the princes, he left his lands undefended. His father-in-law Tugorkhan raided Pereyaslavl, and the Cuman khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniak">Boniak</a> raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and sacked three monasteries, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-pechersk-lavra">Kiev Monastery of the Caves</a>. Tugorkhan was killed in the raid, and Sviatopolk had him buried in Kiev. In 1111 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> led an army that defeated a Cuman army at the Battle of the Salnytsia River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sviatopolk's Christian name was Michael, and he encouraged the embellishment of St Michael's Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, known since as the Golden-Roofed. The history now called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> was compiled by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> during his reign. He married three times, the last time in 1104 to Barbara Komnena. His daughter Zbyslava married Bolesław III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1102 and Predslava married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos-duke-of-croatia">Prince Álmos</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1104. His son Iaroslav, Prince of Volynia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a>, died in 1123, and because of his early death Iaroslav's descendants lost any claim to the Kievan throne and kept Turov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinsk">Pinsk</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sviatopolk II of Kiev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was long attached to Kiev, but in the late 1150s Yuri Yaroslavich, a grandson of <b>Sviatopolk II</b>, made it independent, founding a local dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-turov-and-pinsk">Principality of Turov and Pinsk</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Vyshhorod, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.58,30.49&amp;year=1090" aria-label="Vyshhorod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5245 407 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sviatopolk II of Kiev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5695 677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5695 677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.58,30.49&amp;year=1090">What was here in 1090? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
